                        "entry_id": "36f2649d-0964-3041-8fd9-1ab229e68e01",
                        "glosses": [
                            "In a proleptic manner; anticipatorily."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "a9779906-cb8f-4630-8d03-0b91ac9193d8",
                                "sense_id": "81de0871-849f-3870-a34f-bdd6ddaba2a8",
                                "text": "When we name an event, calling it fire, we speak proleptically; we do not name an immediate event; that is impossible. We employ a term of discourse; we invoke a meaning, namely, the potential consequence of the existence."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "57353ac5-9184-4927-a5da-e0edb8854865",
                                "sense_id": "81de0871-849f-3870-a34f-bdd6ddaba2a8",
                                "text": "The ultimacy of God's eschatological revelation impinges proleptically on . . . and, then, it all gets over my head."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "c562a3da-7625-4038-8326-226bc50c2e40",
                                "sense_id": "81de0871-849f-3870-a34f-bdd6ddaba2a8",
                                "text": "Fullerton, in real life apparently a magnetic, equal opportunity Lothario[…]is written here[…] as more of a puppy than a hound, making campy references to Wharton by her childhood name, Pussy Jones, and proleptically quoting Mae West."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
